class:: RhythmPattern
summary:: Bark/log-power modulation pattern for rhythmic timbre control
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Rhythm
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/RhythmTransform, Classes/BarkBands, Classes/BeatHistogram

description::
Extracts a band-by-modulation-frequency matrix from Bark-band power. MIR2 applies log1p compression, Hann-windowed modulation analysis, optional emphasis around 4 Hz, separable Gaussian smoothing and L2 normalization. Changes in rhythmic activity across frequency bands can control an effect or synthesized accompaniment.

This is a bounded MIR2 adaptation inspired by Lidy and Rauber, “Evaluation of Feature Extractors and Psycho-Acoustic Transformations for Music Genre Classification” (ISMIR 2005), link::https://ismir2005.ismir.net/proceedings/1033.pdf::. It does not reproduce the canonical Rhythm Patterns descriptor: spectral masking, calibrated phon/sone conversion, gradient filtering and whole-track median aggregation are omitted. Neither the output nor its weighting is calibrated in psychoacoustic units. No genre-classification accuracy is claimed. The krBands method permits externally prepared envelopes.

Timing, warmup, held-input pooling and work bounds follow link::Classes/RhythmTransform::. Wait for a full history before expecting modulation results. At most 1024 matrix elements and 524288 band-by-bin-by-history products are accepted. Normalize removes absolute level; use code::\none:: when level matters. The closest vanilla analysis tool is FFT; RhythmPattern adds a Bark/log-power frontend and a bounded matrix of modulation features, rather than an audio-frequency spectrum.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. BarkBands reads each fresh FFT frame and holds the resulting envelope between frames. Choose an FFT hop fast enough to represent the desired modulation rates.
argument:: numBands
Literal Bark-band count, 4–32; default 24. Triangular, weight-normalized Bark filters use power input.
argument:: numBins
Literal modulation-bin count per band, 2–128; default 16. Total output channels must not exceed 1024.
argument:: minHz
Lowest modulation frequency, at least zero; default 0.5 Hz.
argument:: maxHz
Highest modulation frequency; default 8 Hz. Must exceed minHz and be no more than 0.4 times the requested and actual feature rates.
argument:: windowTime
Trailing history, 0.25–12 seconds; default 4. Rounded upward to whole feature frames. Outputs stay zero until the full history has arrived.
argument:: hopTime
Matrix update interval, 0.02–2 seconds; default 0.1. Rounded upward to whole feature frames.
argument:: featureRate
Requested envelope rate, 20–200 Hz; default 100. Average pooling spans round(controlRate / featureRate) control blocks, at least one. Actual timing uses the resulting rate.
argument:: gain
Nonnegative literal gain before log1p compression, at most 1e6; default 1. Compression is log(1 + gain * max(power, 0)).
argument:: normalization
Literal code::\none::, code::\global:: or code::\band::. The latter modes give unit L2 norm to the whole matrix or each band respectively; silence stays zero. Default code::\global::.
argument:: fluctuation
Literal 1 weights modulation frequency f by 2 / ((f / 4) + (4 / f)), with zero at DC and one at 4 Hz. Literal 0 disables this emphasis. Default 1.
argument:: smoothBands
Gaussian standard deviation across band indices, 0–3; default 0.5. Zero disables. A radius of ceil(3 sigma) is used with truncated-edge renormalization.
argument:: smoothBins
Gaussian standard deviation across modulation-bin indices, 0–3; default 0.5. Zero disables. Smoothing follows weighting, before normalization.
argument:: minFreq
Lowest audio frequency for the Bark frontend; default 20 Hz.
argument:: maxFreq
Highest audio frequency; zero uses Nyquist. Default zero.
argument:: fftSize
FFT size, power of two from 256–8192, matching chain; default 1024.

returns::
A flat band-major Array of numBands * numBins nonnegative magnitudes. Use code::values.clump(numBins):: to form rows. No phase channels are produced. All parameters except chain or envelope values are fixed at construction.

method:: krBands
Accepts already prepared band envelopes. Shared arguments have the same meaning as kr; no Bark frontend is inserted.
argument:: bands
Literal Array of 1–32 scalar or control-rate band envelopes. Nonfinite samples become zero; values are bounded to plus/minus 1e12.
argument:: numBins
As in kr.
argument:: minHz
As in kr.
argument:: maxHz
As in kr.
argument:: windowTime
As in kr.
argument:: hopTime
As in kr.
argument:: featureRate
As in kr.
argument:: compression
Literal code::\none:: (default) for externally prepared envelopes, or code::\logPower:: for log(1 + gain * max(input, 0)). Mean subtraction is always enabled.
argument:: gain
As in kr; used only with logPower compression.
argument:: normalization
As in kr.
argument:: fluctuation
As in kr.
argument:: smoothBands
As in kr.
argument:: smoothBins
As in kr.

returns::
The same matrix layout as kr, with bands.size rows.

method:: frequencies
Returns the modulation-frequency grid for minHz, maxHz and numBins. Defaults match kr.

examples::
A repeating sample drives a second filtered layer. The relative modulation activity of the upper Bark bands opens its filter. The first feature update occurs after 1.5 seconds.
code::
(
Routine({
    var buffer = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.child);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2RhythmPatternExample = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, buffer, BufRateScale.kr(buffer), loop: 1)
            * SinOsc.kr(3).range(0.1, 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var matrix = RhythmPattern.kr(chain, numBands: 8, windowTime: 1.5).clump(16);
        var activity = matrix.copyRange(4, 7).flat.sum / (matrix.flat.sum + 1e-9);
        var filtered = RLPF.ar(source, activity.clip(0, 1).lag(0.15).linexp(0, 1, 150, 6000), 0.3);
        [source * 0.3, filtered * 0.2] * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;
    ~mir2RhythmPatternBuffer = buffer;
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
Stop and release:
code::
(
~mir2RhythmPatternExample.free;
~mir2RhythmPatternBuffer.free;
)
::

